<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>There are two shapes, and which one you are in is not your choice.</strong> Front-and-back when you are hitting down, side-by-side when you have had to hit up. The hard part is switching the moment the rally changes hands, and knowing who goes where with a partner you have never played with. Forced to defend: whoever was at the front picks a side, whoever was at the back takes the other.</p>

      <p>Get that wrong and you already know how it goes: the shuttle drops in the gap between you, you both watch it land, and neither of you says anything.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Two formations.</strong> Front-and-back attacking, side-by-side defending. Coaching sources that inflate this to four or five are counting something else, or citing nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>Shuttle down, you attack. Shuttle up, you defend.</strong> At club level that rule has an exception worth knowing about.</li>
        <li><strong>Forced to defend?</strong> Front player picks a side, back player takes the other. It needs no agreement, so it works with a stranger.</li>
        <li><strong>Defending side-by-side is not a straight line.</strong> Slide across towards the shuttle together, and the cross-court player should stand further forward.</li>
        <li><strong>Nobody agrees who takes the middle.</strong> Settle it between you in ten seconds before you start.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club-level doubles with two right-handers of roughly similar standard. Law references are to the Laws of Badminton as at August 2026.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#how-many">Two formations, not four</a>
      <a href="#laws">Where do the Laws say you must stand?</a>
      <a href="#which-shape">How do you tell which shape you should be in?</a>
      <a href="#which-side">Forced to defend: which side do you take?</a>
      <a href="#not-a-line">Defending is not a straight line</a>
      <a href="#middle">Who takes the shuttle down the middle?</a>
      <a href="#serve">Where do you stand for the serve and the return?</a>
      <a href="#partner">What if your partner does not move at all?</a>
      <a href="#does-it-matter">Does any of this matter on a social club night?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="how-many">Two formations, not four</h2>
      <p>Two. The BWF's own coach education says so plainly: the Level 2 player assessment criteria ask a player to <em>"describe 2 doubles formations"</em>, and the Level 1 manual names them, saying pairs <em>"use front and back as their attacking formation and side by side as their defensive formation"</em>. Badminton Bible, TeachPE and the club and academy coaching documents we checked all use the same two.</p>
      <p>You will still see articles promising four formations, or five. Both are counting something other than formations: <strong>four</strong> usually means the two shapes with a left-hand and a right-hand variant each, or the four match-ups you get from two shapes on two sides of the net. Those are useful things to think about; they are not four formations. <strong>Five</strong> generally means an article with no sources at all: one such piece, inventing formations named "diagonal", "hybrid" and "situational", appears word for word across several unrelated domains, and those three names turn up in no coaching material we could find.</p>
      <p>The same applies to the confident percentages that circulate on this subject, whether about rally win rates or collisions avoided. As far as we can tell they trace back to one company's unpublished figures from its own customers, with no method stated and nothing to check. We have no numbers of our own to offer you here, and we would rather say that than borrow someone else's.</p>
      <p>The frame that fits the sources is <strong>two formations and three situations</strong>. The formations are the two shapes. The situations are attacking, defending, and neutral: the flat midcourt exchange where nobody has the attack yet and both pairs are fighting to get it. Neutral is a situation you handle by choosing one of the two shapes, not a third shape of its own.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-attack-defence.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. The attacking pair stand in a line down the middle of their half, one at the net and one at the back. The pair being smashed at stand side by side but not level: both have slid towards the side the shuttle is on, and the one furthest from it stands further forward." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The two shapes. Which one you are in is decided by who is hitting down, not by preference.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="laws">Where do the Laws say you must stand?</h2>
      <p>Almost nowhere, and this surprises people. The Laws constrain the positions of exactly two players, the server and the receiver, and only until the serve is struck. They must <em>"stand within diagonally opposite service courts without touching the boundary lines"</em> (Law 9.1.3) and keep both feet stationary and in contact with the floor until the service is delivered (Law 9.1.4).</p>
      <p>Their partners are free. Law 9.5 is explicit: <em>"in doubles, during the delivery of service, the partners may take up any positions within their respective courts, which do not unsight the opposing server or receiver."</em> So where your partner stands at the serve is entirely a tactical choice, not a rule you are obeying.</p>
      <p>Once the rally is underway, even the server and receiver are released. Law 11.2 says the shuttle may be hit <em>"by either player of the serving side and either player of the receiving side alternately, from any position on that player's side of the net"</em>. The word "alternately" there is about the two sides taking turns, not the two partners: it does not mean you and your partner must take it in turns. Two club misconceptions die on that sentence, because the score does not dictate where you stand during a rally either.</p>

      <h2 id="which-shape">How do you tell which shape you should be in?</h2>
      <p>Look at which way the shuttle is travelling. If your side is hitting it downwards you are attacking, so get into front-and-back. If you have had to hit it upwards you are defending, so get side-by-side. A club coaching document from Cambridge puts the test simply: <em>"you can tell which team is attacking because they will be hitting the shuttle down, whereas the defending team will be hitting it up."</em></p>
      <p>The exception matters more at club level than anywhere else, and the same document names it: <em>"a weak player may hit the shuttle down so slowly and flatly that the opposition are able to smash it."</em> A slow, floaty drop shot travels downwards and still hands over the attack. So the real test is not the direction of your shot, it is whether your opponents can hit down next. If they can, you are defending, whatever you just did.</p>
      <p><strong>Who goes to the front?</strong> Whoever played the shot that made the attack. If you have just dropped or smashed, follow it in and take the net; your partner covers behind you. That way the person already moving forwards keeps moving, rather than the two of you swapping places while the shuttle is in the air.</p>

      <h2 id="which-side">Forced to defend: which side do you take?</h2>
      <p><strong>If you already have a sense of which side your partner is on, take the other one.</strong> Often you do, and no rule is needed. The problem is the other times.</p>
      <p>You were attacking in a line, one at the net and one at the back. You have had to lift. Now you both need to be side-by-side, immediately, with no time to discuss it.</p>
      <p>When you do not, <strong>the player who was at the front picks a side, either side, and the player who was at the back takes the other one.</strong> It works because it is asymmetric: the player at the back can see their partner in front of them and can see which way they went, while the front player cannot see behind. So the person with the information is the one who adapts.</p>
      <p>That protocol is set out in the teaching document of Graduates' Badminton Club in Cambridge (2011), and its virtue is that it needs no agreement in advance. You can use it with someone you met four minutes ago, which at a club night is often the situation. If there is no time even to choose deliberately, the same document has the fallback: <em>"just pick any side, and your partner, who can see which choice you made, will go to the other side."</em></p>
      <p>Worth being straight about the evidence here: this is one club's written practice, not a governing-body instruction, and it is a decade and a half old. We are recommending it because the reasoning holds and because it survives the club-night test that fancier systems fail, not because anyone official has endorsed it.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-who-takes-which-side.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. A pair who were attacking in a front-and-back line have had to lift. The front player moves to one side and the back player, who can see that choice, moves to the other, finishing side by side." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Whoever can see the other player's decision is the one who adapts. That is why this works without a conversation.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="not-a-line">Defending is not a straight line</h2>
      <p><strong>Start from about the middle of your half, a step behind the short service line, and about a metre in from your tramline.</strong> That is the baseline. From there, two adjustments matter, and almost every diagram you will see of side-by-side defence gets both of them wrong by drawing two players level with each other, each guarding half the width.</p>
      <p><strong>You move across together, not apart.</strong> The BWF Level 1 manual: <em>"for wider lifts, the pair move across together towards the same side as the shuttle has been lifted. This allows them to deal more effectively with straight or down-the-middle replies from the attacker, which are the most frequent returns."</em> You are not each defending a half. You are both shifting towards the danger, because that is where the shuttle is most likely to go.</p>
      <p><strong>And you do not stay level.</strong> The same manual, describing what to do when one attacker is under pressure in a rear corner: <em>"the cross-court player can take up a more advanced position, because cross-court strokes will take longer to reach her, so she will have more time to react."</em> The player furthest from the shuttle has more time, so they can afford to stand further up the court and cover the drop into the middle. The player on the shuttle's side stays wider and deeper, nearer their tramline, because a straight smash arrives fast. How far forward depends on how much pressure the attacker is under: the manual ties it to that, not to a fixed distance, so treat it as a lean rather than a mark on the floor.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-defence-asymmetric.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. The shuttle is being smashed straight down one side. Both defenders have slid across towards it rather than staying level. The defender on the shuttle's side is wide and deep near the tramline; the cross-court defender is nearer the centre line and a step further forward. Faded circles show the level positions most club pairs take instead." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The faded circles are where most club pairs stand. Sliding across together, with the cross-court player further forward, covers the two replies the attacker is most likely to choose.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="middle">Who takes the shuttle down the middle?</h2>
      <p>There is no agreed rule, and you should know that rather than hunt for one. Three positions circulate and they contradict each other.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Position</th><th>The argument</th><th>How well supported</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Forehand takes it</strong></td>
            <td>Easiest to remember, and the most commonly repeated.</td>
            <td>Convention. We found no coaching authority stating it as a rule.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>You defend on the backhand anyway</strong></td>
            <td>Defending a smash aimed at your body with a forehand action is close to impossible, so doubles defence is played predominantly backhand, the middle included.</td>
            <td>The physiology is sound and widely echoed, but the clearest published statements of it are anonymous.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Agree it beforehand</strong></td>
            <td>Decide before the rally who owns the middle when you are defending.</td>
            <td>The advice of coaches who decline to pick between the other two.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Our view: take the third one, because it is the only one that survives being paired with a stranger. Ten seconds before you start, say "I'll take the middle" and let your partner disagree if they would rather. Any agreement beats both of you assuming the other has it, which is how the shuttle ends up landing between you untouched.</p>

      <h2 id="serve">Where do you stand for the serve and the return?</h2>
      <p>Serving side: the server serves low and short from close to the short service line, and stays forward after serving, ready for a reply into the net. Their partner covers the back. You are already in the attacking shape before the rally starts, which is the point of a low serve.</p>
      <p>Receiving side: the receiver stands ready to take the serve early and high, and their partner covers the back. The error that costs most here belongs to the partner rather than the receiver. The Cambridge document is specific about it: when the serve goes high, the problem is <em>"for the receiver's partner to forget to move to the front of the court"</em>, which leaves a large area open on the very next shot.</p>
      <p>If you take one habit from this section, make it that one. When your partner receives, watch what the serve does and move accordingly, rather than watching your partner play the shot.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Worked example: one rally, three changes of shape</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">You serve low, stay forward, and your partner covers the back: <strong>front-and-back</strong>. They push it flat to your midcourt and nobody can hit down: this is the <strong>neutral</strong> situation, and you hold the attacking shape while you have a chance of getting the attack back.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">Your partner has to lift. You are <strong>defending</strong> now, so you pick a side, your partner takes the other, and you both slide towards whichever side the lift went. They smash straight and you block it to the net. Now they have to lift, so you are <strong>attacking</strong> again: you played the block, so you take the net and your partner drops back.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">Three changes of shape in one rally, decided by who could hit down, and not one of them needed a conversation.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="partner">What if your partner does not move at all?</h2>
      <p>Then you use the front-player-picks rule anyway, because it is one-sided by design. You do not need your partner's cooperation to make the right move yourself, and if they stay where they are, you have still covered a different part of the court from them, which is most of the benefit.</p>
      <p><strong>Do not turn round to watch your partner hit.</strong> If you are at the front, keep facing the net. Turning to look means taking a shuttle in the face at close range, which is why coaches keep saying it.</p>
      <p><strong>And talk to each other.</strong> Calling "mine" or "yours" is legal, and worth doing. Law 13.4.5 faults a player who <em>"deliberately distracts an opponent by any action such as shouting or making gestures"</em>. That is about opponents, and about intent, so calling to your own partner is ordinary play.</p>

      <h2 id="does-it-matter">Does any of this matter on a social club night?</h2>
      <p>Less than the internet implies. Badminton England's No Strings programme runs several hundred drop-in sessions a week, explicitly for players of all abilities and built around fun rather than competition, and that is the format most people reading this are actually in. (Their own published figures range from "over 500" on the Badminton England site to around a thousand on the programme's own, which is a good illustration of why we date the numbers we quote.) If you play once a week for enjoyment, the two shapes and the front-player-picks rule are the whole of what you need. The rest of this guide is detail for people who want to compete.</p>
      <p>It is worth noting that the club whose protocol we quoted above deliberately does not teach the professional method. Their document says so, with some cheerfulness: <em>"professional players have a different system, involving anticlockwise rotation, but we aren't that good."</em> A functioning club decided that a simpler rule its members could actually use beat a better rule they could not. That is a sound judgement, not a compromise, and it applies to most club nights.</p>
      <p>Nobody is going to run a drill on a social club night, and you do not need one. Read this once, notice it occasionally, and that is the whole return. Coaches do drill these transitions, and if you join a coached session you will meet them properly, but that is a different evening from the one this guide is about.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">When you are forced to defend, the player who was at the front picks a side and the player who was at the back takes the other. You never have to agree it, discuss it, or know your partner.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Fair games, without the admin</h4>
        <p>Knowing where to stand helps most when the games are even in the first place. ePegboard picks balanced pairings on a club night and keeps the mixing varied, so people play with a range of partners rather than the same three. Free for clubs.</p>
