      <h2 id="related">Where to go next</h2>
      <p>"Rotation" means two different things in badminton, and this guide covered only one of them. If you arrived looking for how clubs decide <em>whose turn it is to play next</em> rather than where to stand on court, that is a separate subject: start with <a href="/guides/badminton-club-night-rotation">fair player rotation on a club night</a>, or <a href="/guides/managing-club-night-rotation">the four ways clubs manage the queue</a>.</p>
      <p>If your games are one-sided rather than badly positioned, no amount of formation work will fix that. <a href="/guides/mixed-ability-badminton-club-night">Running a mixed-ability club night</a> deals with the pairing problem instead, and <a href="/guides/game-variety-stop-same-players">stopping the same four playing together every week</a> covers the variety side of it.</p>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">How this guide was put together</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">ePegboard builds club-night software. We are not coaches and we have not tested any of this in a lab. This guide is compiled from the BWF Coaches' Manual (Levels 1 and 2), the Laws of Badminton, and published teaching material from coaches and clubs, with the sources named in the text so you can check them. Where the published sources disagree, or where we could not find one at all, we have said so rather than picking an answer.</p>
